 "cushionNotice": "No single row is the story. Stack them. Ecuador's cushions are often written down (free public health care in the Constitution, a disability transfer, a pension for the poorest old) and thin in practice: a hospital at 40% of its medicines, $100 a month at 65. Colorado's are often thick and behind a door: a W-2, $2,500 in reported wages, a mortgage. A Center family meets a broken arm, a 14-hour blackout or a death with almost nothing underneath: no contract, no IESS, no paid leave, no insurance, a house that is also the savings account. Most RJ families meet the same events with five or six cushions they did not build and rarely see. Quito is not a place without cushions: its adequate-employment rate is half again the national rate. The cushion exists. The question is who it reaches.",
 "fences": [
  {
   "h": "Insulation",
   "text": "Paid leave, health insurance, a bank that will lend, a wage floor that is enforced. These are invisible from inside, so another family's fall looks like a choice. See 2c. Ecuador uses the US dollar, which makes it worse: $482 looks like a number we understand. It isn't. A month's minimum wage there is about 25 hours at Denver's minimum *(our arithmetic: 482 ÷ 19.29)*. Prices in Quito are not a quarter of Denver's."
  },
  {
   "h": "What we didn't notice",
   "text": "A home lit by one candle was never connected to a grid that failed. What students noticed was what can be seen in one afternoon: a one-room house, a long workday, a mother's hopes for her children. Barriers that work through a weekly payment, a schedule or a rule are invisible from a home visit. Ask before the trip: *what here would I not see even if it were in front of me?*"
  },
  {
   "h": "Physical",
   "text": "Students sleep on the Center's campus in the Community House, travel as a group, and follow a safety orientation (c4wf.org FAQ ✅). The security rules are prudent, and they are also a fence: the neighborhood is seen from a van window. Visitors' Quito is the colonial center, the equator monument and the *teleférico*, not the lanes behind the Center."
  },
  {
   "h": "Cognitive",
   "text": "Informal heard as illegal; child labor as one thing, and a working child as proof of a bad parent rather than of a budget that doesn't close; Ecuador as Galápagos and bananas; expecting Quito to be hot and humid; the security crisis as *cartels on the coast, not here* or as Colombia's and Peru's fault, leaving out cocaine demand abroad, Ecuador's ports and its prisons. Not knowing that the Center's families ran this place for sixty years before we arrived, so what we see looks like how it has always been."
  },
  {
   "h": "Affective",
   "text": "Fear: security news can turn every street corner into a threat and every young man into a suspect. Pre-trip letters called Quito unsafe and expected resentment from the children; instead, children ran to hug them on day one. Then the **rescue impulse**: expecting to teach, fix or be needed, and being served empanadas first; hoping the families are grateful the group came. Pity for children who once worked. Judging the house on arrival. Hearing about absent parents produces judgment. Guilt on the way home, then the trip fading fast in Denver. Keep three things apart: what you saw, what you were told and by whom, and what you concluded."
  },
  {
   "h": "Romanticizing",
   "text": "Seeing families as free from consumer pressure, as having what we have lost, and explaining generosity and joy as traits of the poor. Comparing everything to home (a coffee drink, a $15 lunch, a phone) can turn families into a lesson for us. Both the deficit portrait and the romantic one keep the structure out of view."
  },
  {
   "h": "Theological",
   "text": "We're so blessed, reading our cushions as God's favor. And the subtler fence this partner sets up: the Center's language of personal responsibility (rules, savings, not a charity) can let us conclude that poverty is a moral failure the poor must fix. Its own founder built a structure of meals, school, clinic and minga *because* effort alone wasn't enough. Both are true. Hold them together. Students more often made the opposite move and told the Center as a **rescue** that saved a family, leaving out the families' own savings, membership and work, which are part of the Center's model."
  }
 ],
