   "question": "A family's budget doesn't close this week. What does a 13-year-old's afternoon cost, and what does it earn?",
   "changes": "household income vs. the minimum wage; one adult sick or out of work; rent due; what the child can earn after school (street sale, family stall, piecework); hours of school missed; whether a program feeds the child and covers the gap; whether anyone checks on the hours.",
   "data": "child labor 7.01% of ages 5–14 (2024, ENEMDU); children's earnings once up to 85% of household income in the Center's families (2009, ⚠️ old); Ecuador's minimum wage and informality (Barrier 1).",
   "notice": "in some weeks the child's income is what keeps the family housed. The law can forbid the work; only income, school and food together replace it.",
   "yourCall": "If you could change one thing — the law, the school day, the family's income, or the program — which would actually keep her in school, and who would pay for it?",
